COMMIT 4cbc12112e066b3e751e9e79a653f8dc17634c97
fix(runtime): FIX-62 unstarted clears the flows it discarded out of built

`unstarted` stops the flows that did not start on restore but left them in
`built`, so the catch at the end of the install path would have discarded the
same flows a second time. It now removes them as it discards them.

The defect is not reachable from a test, and this is recorded rather than
glossed: after `unstarted` runs, nothing in that span can throw — the records
save is skipped on restore, `previous` stays null because `restore()` refuses to
run unless `deployed` is empty, the `kept.computeIfAbsent` lambda never runs
because every flow carries the one archive's sha256, and `new Deployed`,
`Map.copyOf` and `List.copyOf` get only non-null arguments there. The reviewer
re-walked that span and reached the same conclusion independently.

So the change goes in on the strength of the document, not of a measurement.
The javadoc of `rollBack` states the invariant outright — "`built` holds what a
throw would leave to stop wherever one can happen: discard never throws, so a
list it stopped is cleared before anything that may throw runs" — and the code
contradicted it. The point of a rule like that is that nobody has to redo the
reachability analysis the next time this method changes. Same call as FIX-60's
out.close() path.

Neither new test is a red: both pass against the unfixed code, by design.
`aFlowThatDoesNotStartOnRestoreIsStoppedOnceAndTheOthersNotAtAll` is a guard —
`Door.stop()` is not idempotent, so a second discard would append another
`stop`/`stopped` to the history and a second `stop shop/b` event, which it
asserts; it observes a double discard, it just cannot be driven to one.
`aFlowThatDoesNotStartOnRestoreLogsOneErrorNamingItAndItsCause` pins the full
ERROR text, which was correct but unasserted — the older cases only checked that
the package name appeared. The comparison is against whole literal messages, so
the `shop/b` versus `shop/bb` substring trap that bit FIX-54 and FIX-121 twice
more cannot return.

Checks: VERIFIED_PASS exit 0, clean verify -Pit in the lane2 worktree, BUILD
SUCCESS, 17 modules, ~2400 tests, 0 failures / 0 errors / 0 skipped
(agent/work/FIX-62-verify-2.log); coverage check exit 0, "coverage: no decrease"
(FIX-62-coverage.log); DeploymentsRestoreTest 13/0.

NOT_RUN — the double discard itself. Deleting the fix leaves all 410 scoped
tests green (FIX-62-green-mutant.log), so the line is a confirmed equivalent
mutant and no test can kill it. The ERROR pin does kill mutants on the log
statement, so the pair is not decorative.

An earlier run of the same command was ENVIRONMENT_BLOCKED, not failing: dockerd
had died at 05:31 leaving a stale pid file, and the Postgres cluster was down.
Both were restarted and the four unexplained messages in that log turned out to
be warnings printed by tests that pass, one of them the known CPU-sampler read
(F-22, FIX-108).

Integration note: committed in the lane2 worktree as f318124 and brought onto
this branch by applying its runtime half path-scoped, because a cherry-pick
needs a clean tree and FIX-71 is in flight in `flottila-core` here. Both files
were compared with `cmp` against f318124 and are byte-identical, so this commit
carries that commit's content and nothing else. The evidence above is the lane
worktree's run; the integrated tree's own `verify -Pit` arrives with the FIX-71
commit, which is the next code commit on this branch.

